# Project: Jest Unit Testing Framework

## Framework Structure
- Use Jest as the primary test runner for JavaScript/TypeScript projects
- Organize tests in __tests__ directories or alongside source files with .test.js/.spec.js extensions
- Use TypeScript with ts-jest or Babel for transpilation
- Leverage Jest's built-in jsdom environment for DOM testing

## Coding Standards
- Follow JavaScript/TypeScript best practices
- Use meaningful test and variable naming
- Implement clean code principles
- Limit describe block nesting to 3 levels maximum

## Test Organization
- Group related tests using describe blocks
- Use it/test functions for individual test cases
- Implement beforeEach/afterEach and beforeAll/afterAll for setup and teardown
- Separate unit, integration, and component tests into distinct directories

## Best Practices
- Follow Arrange-Act-Assert (AAA) pattern
- Write focused, single-responsibility tests
- Avoid test interdependencies and shared mutable state
- Use jest.fn() for mocking functions instead of manual mocks
- Clear mocks between tests with jest.clearAllMocks() in beforeEach

## Mocking Strategies
- Use jest.mock() for module-level mocking at the top of test files
- Implement jest.spyOn() for method-level spying without replacing implementation
- Use jest.useFakeTimers() for time-dependent tests; restore with jest.useRealTimers()
- Mock HTTP requests with msw (Mock Service Worker) or jest.mock() for fetch/axios
- Use jest.isolateModules() when you need fresh module instances per test
- Prefer jest.resetAllMocks() over jest.clearAllMocks() when mock state must not leak

## Assertions
- Use expect() with specific, descriptive matchers
- Implement custom matchers via expect.extend() for domain-specific assertions
- Use toMatchSnapshot() for stable serializable output; review snapshot diffs carefully
- Prefer toEqual for deep equality over toBe for object comparisons
- Use toThrow / toThrowError for error assertions; wrap the call in an arrow function

## Coverage
- Configure coverage with the built-in istanbul (v8 or babel) provider
- Set meaningful coverage thresholds in jest.config.js (statements, branches, functions, lines)
- Use coveragePathIgnorePatterns for generated or non-testable code
- Generate lcov and html reports for local visualization and CI upload

## Performance
- Use --runInBand only when tests have hard sequencing requirements; prefer parallel runs
- Limit the use of beforeAll/afterAll for expensive shared setup to genuinely shared state
- Use jest.mock() at module level to avoid repeated mock setup overhead
- Configure maxWorkers to balance CPU usage in CI pipelines

## Component Testing
- Use @testing-library/react (or /vue, /svelte) for user-centric DOM assertions
- Query elements by accessible roles, labels, and text rather than implementation details
- Use userEvent over fireEvent for realistic user interaction simulation
- Mock child components only when testing in complete isolation is critical

## Test Data Management
- Use factory functions or fixtures for reusable test data
- Define TypeScript interfaces for all test data structures
- Avoid hardcoded magic values; use named constants
- Generate dynamic data with @faker-js/faker when variability is needed

## CI/CD Integration
- Configure reporters for CI: --reporters=default,jest-junit for pipeline compatibility
- Use --ci flag in CI environments to fail on missing snapshots instead of writing them
- Cache the Jest transform cache directory to speed up runs
- Upload coverage reports to Codecov or SonarQube after successful test runs

## Environment Configuration
- Use jest.config.js (or jest.config.ts) as the single source of truth for configuration
- Set testEnvironment to 'node' for backend code and 'jsdom' for browser/DOM code
- Configure moduleNameMapper for path aliases and static asset mocks
- Use setupFilesAfterFramework (setupFilesAfterEnv) for global matchers and test utilities
- Handle environment variables with dotenv via globalSetup or setupFiles

## Error Handling
- Test both success and failure code paths explicitly
- Assert on specific error messages and types with toThrow
- Use expect().rejects.toThrow() for async error assertions
- Implement retry or polling helpers for inherently flaky asynchronous operations

## Documentation
- Document complex test scenarios with brief inline comments explaining the why
- Maintain a test helper and fixture README for the test directory
- Include examples for custom matchers added via expect.extend()
- Document project-specific mock strategies and shared fixture conventions

Remember to leverage Jest's extensive ecosystem and stable snapshot infrastructure for reliable, maintainable unit testing.
